<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
    <groupId>com.poc.ansa</groupId>
    <artifactId>poc-ansa-biotech</artifactId>
    <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <packaging>mule-application</packaging>

    <properties>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
        <app.runtime>4.6.0</app.runtime>
        <!--
          Tried bumping this from the brief's 3.3.0 to 3.7.3 (the latest
          version available for munit-runner, munit-tools, and
          munit-maven-plugin alike on repository.mulesoft.org/releases,
          bumped together to stay in lockstep) to test whether
          munit-maven-plugin 3.3.0 wasn't hooking correctly into
          mule-maven-plugin 4.10.1's packaging lifecycle. It did not help:
          3.7.3 fails earlier and differently (a JPMS module conflict,
          "Module reactor.core reads more than one module named
          plexus.component.annotations", before the embedded container
          even starts) rather than fixing the original silent
          zero-tests-discovered problem. Reverted to 3.3.0, which is the
          furthest-progressing combination found (clean deploy, but MUnit
          still discovers zero tests for reasons undiagnosed from inside
          this environment; see Task 5's report). Found while executing
          Task 5.
        -->
        <munit.version>3.3.0</munit.version>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.mule.connectors</groupId>
            <artifactId>mule-http-connector</artifactId>
            <version>1.9.0</version>
            <classifier>mule-plugin</classifier>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.mule.connectors</groupId>
            <artifactId>mule-jms-connector</artifactId>
            <version>1.9.2</version>
            <classifier>mule-plugin</classifier>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.apache.activemq</groupId>
            <artifactId>artemis-jms-client-all</artifactId>
            <version>2.37.0</version>
        </dependency>
        <!--
          Brief specified groupId org.mule.modules (plural) and version
          1.2.19; neither resolves. Real groupId is org.mule.module
          (singular, confirmed via MuleSoft's own Java Module docs and the
          Anypoint Exchange metadata listing). 1.2.19 doesn't exist in that
          listing at all (it jumps 1.2.18 -> 2.0.x), so pinned to the
          nearest valid release. Found while executing Task 5.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.mule.module</groupId>
            <artifactId>mule-java-module</artifactId>
            <!--
              Version-hunted extensively while executing Task 12 to find a
              release of this connector that both builds AND deploys inside
              the real Mule 4.6.0 standalone runtime:
                - 1.2.18 (brief's nearest-valid-release from Task 5): builds
                  fine, but deploy throws EnumConstantNotPresentException:
                  org.mule.sdk.api.meta.JavaVersion.JAVA_25 (its extension
                  model declares JAVA_25 as a supported Java version in its
                  bundled annotation metadata, but the mule-sdk-api actually
                  loaded by the runtime's core classloader at deploy time
                  predates that enum constant, so reflectively reading the
                  annotation throws).
                - 1.2.15/1.2.16 don't even resolve: their POMs depend on
                  org.springframework:spring-core:5.3.39-spring-framework-5.3.45,
                  an internal MuleSoft-forked version string not published
                  on any repository this project can reach (confirmed 404 on
                  both repository.mulesoft.org/releases and
                  anypoint-exchange-v3).
                - 1.2.13, the newest release still on a plain published
                  spring-core (5.3.33), resolves and builds, but deploy fails
                  differently: "Can't resolve .../mule-ee.xsd" plus a
                  suppressed "No enum constant ...ComponentType.SUB_FLOW"
                  (its jar bundles mule-sdk-api 0.7.0 / mule-extensions-api
                  1.5.0, both older than what the 4.6.0 runtime's own config
                  parser uses (1.6.0 / mule-artifact-ast-serialization
                  1.2.0), clashing with app-wide XML parsing for every
                  config file, not just the one using java:invoke-static).
                - 1.2.10 avoids both of the above but is rejected outright:
                  JavaVersionNotSupportedByExtensionException, "Supported
                  versions are: [1.8, 11]" (no 17, and this runtime's JRE is
                  17).
              No publicly-resolvable version threads this needle, so stayed
              on 1.2.18 (verified the closest to a clean deploy of all of
              the above). Also tried fixing the JAVA_25 problem at what
              looked like its root: the 4.6.0 distribution's own bundled
              mule-sdk-api (0.8.0, confirmed via its JavaVersion.class:
              only JAVA_8/11/17) predates that enum constant, and swapping
              in a newer mule-sdk-api release that has it (tried both
              1.0.0, the earliest with JAVA_25, and 1.2.0, the latest) does
              make that specific exception go away. But it trades it for a
              worse one: empirically (confirmed by toggling only this one
              change, everything else held fixed), doing so breaks the
              distribution's own EE transform module's schema
              registration, so deploy then fails on the first config file
              using ee:transform (nearly every file in this app) instead of
              the one using java:invoke-static. No sdk-api version
              satisfies both: every release with JAVA_25 breaks EE
              registration, and every release that doesn't predates
              JAVA_25. Left mule-app/Dockerfile NOT patching sdk-api as a
              result: JAVA_25 (blocking java:invoke, i.e. this app's whole
              Salesforce JWT auth path) remains the app's current,
              unresolved deploy blocker on this runtime; see Task 12's
              report for the full diagnostic trail and what's ruled out.
            -->
            <version>1.2.18</version>
            <classifier>mule-plugin</classifier>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.mulesoft.munit</groupId>
            <artifactId>munit-runner</artifactId>
            <version>${munit.version}</version>
            <classifier>mule-plugin</classifier>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.mulesoft.munit</groupId>
            <artifactId>munit-tools</artifactId>
            <version>${munit.version}</version>
            <classifier>mule-plugin</classifier>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <!--
              Bumped from the brief's 4.2.0. That version's process-classes
              goal crashes with EnumConstantNotPresentException:
              JavaVersion.JAVA_25 as soon as the project has any
              src/main/java sources (JwtSigner.java here) on this JDK,
              regardless of which mule-java-module version is used;
              tried both 1.2.18 and 2.0.6, same crash either way, so it is
              this plugin's own tooling, not the java-module dependency.
              Bumped to the latest available release to pick up a fix.
              Found while executing Task 5.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.mule.tools.maven</groupId>
                <artifactId>mule-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <!--
                  4.10.1 (the version bumped to in order to get past a
                  build-time crash in the brief's original 4.2.0, see
                  below) builds fine, but its generated
                  META-INF/mule-artifact/artifact.ast doesn't match this
                  Mule 4.6.0 runtime's own component/parameter models at
                  deploy time in several different ways (a SUB_FLOW enum
                  constant the runtime doesn't have, an "encoding" parameter
                  on <configuration-properties> it doesn't recognize, and
                  further unresolvable nested models beyond that), found
                  while debugging Task 12's Docker deploy; setting
                  runtimeVersion/skipValidation didn't change what it
                  embeds. mule-maven-plugin 4.6.0 exists as an exact
                  version-matched release for this runtime, on the theory
                  it'd generate an AST format the runtime actually
                  understands. (The SUB_FLOW and "encoding" problems above
                  were actually fixed separately, at the content level,
                  before this plugin version was even tried: see
                  salesforce-system-api.xml's comment on dropping its
                  source-less sub-flow/flow-ref, and global-config.xml's on
                  dropping <configuration-properties>. Whether 4.6.0 alone,
                  without those content changes, would also have avoided
                  those two specific errors was never isolated; left as an
                  open question, and the content changes are correct to
                  keep regardless of plugin version.) This plugin bump does
                  NOT fix the
                  deeper, still-unresolved mule-java-module/mule-sdk-api
                  conflict described in that dependency's own comment above:
                  deploy currently fails there before ever reaching this
                  plugin's AST at all, so whether 4.6.0 fully resolves the
                  AST-format mismatch on its own is unconfirmed beyond that
                  point. Left at 4.6.0 regardless since it's the more
                  defensible choice (version-matched to the runtime) once
                  the java-module blocker is resolved.
                -->
                <version>4.6.0</version>
                <extensions>true</extensions>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>com.mulesoft.munit.tools</groupId>
                <artifactId>munit-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${munit.version}</version>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>test</id>
                        <phase>test</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>test</goal>
                            <goal>coverage-report</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <munitTests>
                                <munitTest>**/*-test-suite.xml</munitTest>
                            </munitTests>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
            <!--
              The mule-application packaging's default lifecycle still binds
              a plain maven-surefire-plugin "default-test" execution to the
              test phase (for projects with Java-based unit tests under
              src/test/java; this project's src/main/java has JwtSigner.java
              but there are no Java tests under src/test/java). With no Java
              test sources that execution is normally a harmless no-op, but
              `-Dtest=<suite-name>` on the command line (per this task's run
              command) is a generic Surefire filter property that this
              unrelated execution also picks up and then fails on ("No tests
              matching pattern"), aborting the build before the
              munit-maven-plugin execution above ever runs. Telling it not
              to fail when a -Dtest filter matches nothing keeps it a no-op
              as intended. (Found while executing Task 5 the first time;
              still needed after the Salesforce-connector-to-REST rewrite.)
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <configuration>
                    <failIfNoSpecifiedTests>false</failIfNoSpecifiedTests>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

    <repositories>
        <repository>
            <id>anypoint-exchange-v3</id>
            <name>Anypoint Exchange</name>
            <url>https://maven.anypoint.mulesoft.com/api/v3/maven</url>
        </repository>
        <!--
          The brief's Step 2 only lists mulesoft-releases as a
          pluginRepository, but munit-runner/munit-tools (regular
          test-scoped dependencies, not plugins) 3.3.0 only resolve
          anonymously from repository.mulesoft.org/releases, not from
          anypoint-exchange-v3 (confirmed by probing both directly). Found
          while executing Task 5 the first time; still needed.
        -->
        <repository>
            <id>mulesoft-releases</id>
            <name>MuleSoft Releases Repository</name>
            <url>https://repository.mulesoft.org/releases/</url>
        </repository>
    </repositories>
    <pluginRepositories>
        <pluginRepository>
            <id>mulesoft-releases</id>
            <name>MuleSoft Releases Repository</name>
            <url>https://repository.mulesoft.org/releases/</url>
        </pluginRepository>
    </pluginRepositories>
</project>
